## Interlude / Side B — English Translation: Chapters 17–20

################################################################################
## CHAPTER 17 — Iron Rhythms and Dark Broth
################################################################################

translate en b17_001:
    "That afternoon, the sound hunt led them to an old railway crossing cutting straight through a crowded neighborhood. The drizzle that had hung in the air since noon was starting to turn into real rain."

translate en b17_002a:
    "Kai stood beside Yara, hugging the waterproof camera bag to his chest while holding the second recorder in his hand — his official job ever since he'd said, \"Starting tomorrow, I'm coming with you.\""

translate en b17_003a:
    yara "{i}(whispers, adjusting her recorder){/i} Keep quiet for a second. Listen. The trains on this line run old diesel engines. The clack of the wheels hitting the rail joints is heavy, uneven… It's one of my favorite sounds."

translate en b17_004a:
    kai "You even memorized the engine?"

translate en b17_005a:
    yara "My second pair of ears doesn't get to ask so many questions. {i}(smiles, angling the recorder in Kai's hand){/i} Tilt it slightly toward the tracks. There. Like that."

translate en b17_006a:
    kai_hati "Second pair of ears. New nickname. Just the other day I was \"the Boy Waiting to Die,\" and now I've got a slight promotion."

translate en b17_002b:
    "For this one, Yara hadn't invited Kai. She recorded by herself along the tracks — and as usual, she never asked twice."

translate en b17_003b:
    yara_hati "Old diesel. Heavy, uneven rhythm. Still sounds great on my own. {i}(pause){/i} …Just usually someone's here to hold the bag."

translate en b17_002c:
    "Yara recorded alone that afternoon. Her mic pointed toward the darkening, rain-wet tracks, and she waited patiently like a hunter who knew her quarry was bound to pass."

translate en b17_003c:
    yara_hati "Record it first. He can listen to the finished track later."

translate en b17_007:
    "A shrill horn split the sound of the rain. The ground hummed under their feet. A faded red-and-blue locomotive rolled slowly past, hauling freight cars that shrieked along the rails. The clang of iron on iron was thick and raw — blending with the hoarse diesel rumble and rain hammering the warung's tin roof."

translate en b17_008a:
    yara "{i}(lowers her mic, looking satisfied){/i} Got it. Super clean. {i}(pats Kai's shoulder){/i} Nice work, Second Pair of Ears."

translate en b17_009a:
    kai "I literally just held it, Yar."

translate en b17_010a:
    yara "Holding it is the hardest part. Tilt it off by a hair and the whole track bleeds."

translate en b17_008b:
    "The train pulled away, its rumble fading behind the curtain of rain. Yara lowered her mic, satisfied. Only then did Kai show up from the direction of the warung, half-soaked, carrying two folding umbrellas."

translate en b17_009b:
    kai "…I'm late, aren't I?"

translate en b17_010b:
    yara "Train just passed. {i}(holds up her recorder){/i} Got the sound, though. Just sit — I was about to order food anyway."

translate en b17_011:
    "From inside the warung, a rich, savory smell had been teasing them for a while: thick broth simmered with kluwek and sharp spices. Kai's stomach growled before he even got a chance to order."

translate en b17_012:
    yara "See, this is the best part of hunting sounds in the rain."

translate en b17_013:
    pemilik_warung "Two rawon, Nduk? Sambal on the side?"

translate en b17_014a:
    yara "We'll eat here, Cak. Might as well wait out the rain."

translate en b17_015a:
    pemilik_warung "You got it. {i}(heaps more sambal than usual onto their bowls){/i} Gave you extra sambal — gotta stay warm in rain like this. Last train's at eight-thirty, so you've got plenty of time if you're hanging around."

translate en b17_016a:
    "Two steaming bowls of rawon sat on the wooden table alongside tempe mendol and prawn crackers. Kai spooned in a little sambal, slowly stirring the dark broth and letting the steam warm his face."

translate en b17_017a:
    yara "{i}(takes a bite){/i} Good?"

translate en b17_018a:
    kai "{i}(nods honestly){/i} So good. Warms you right down to your chest."

translate en b17_019a:
    yara "{i}(smiles faintly){/i} The rain, the train rhythm, and a hot bowl of rawon. The most perfect composition today could possibly make."

translate en b17_020a:
    "They ate in comfortable silence beside the tracks, with rain pattering into the puddles between the gravel stones."

translate en b17_014b:
    kai "Two to go, Cak. One for me, one for… someone at the shop who hasn't eaten yet."

translate en b17_015b:
    yara "{i}(raises an eyebrow){/i} Going to eat at Pak Ageng's shop? Alright then. I'll stay here — there's one more freight train I want to wait for."

translate en b17_016b:
    ageng "{i}(eyes the takeout bags in Kai's hand){/i} Lho. What'd you bring there, kid?"

translate en b17_017b:
    kai "Rawon, Pak. From that warung by the tracks. Still warm. Eat with me, Pak — it's way too much for just me."

translate en b17_018b:
    ageng "{i}(chuckles, grabbing bowls from a small shelf){/i} This kid. Everywhere you go, you're looking out for an old man's stomach. Alright, bring it here. Let's eat."

translate en b17_019b:
    "Takeout rawon, eaten between shelves of cassettes with rain falling out in the alley. It tasted just as good."

translate en b17_021:
    kai_hati "Weird, huh. Just sitting here eating like this… I haven't thought once about what tomorrow's going to be like. Don't need a cassette to tell me if today's good or bad. Today just is good. Period."

translate en b17_022c:
    "Kai hadn't gone to record the train. He only heard the finished track — the next afternoon, at that same warung, playing from the tiny speaker on Yara's recorder."

translate en b17_023c:
    kai "…All of that was yesterday? In the rain?"

translate en b17_024c:
    yara "Yeah. Shame you weren't there. In person it's way more… {i}(shrugs){/i} well, that's just how recordings are. You only ever get to hear the real thing once, right when it happens."

translate en b17_025c:
    kai_hati "It sounds great. Still feels distant, though. Like listening to somebody else's life from behind a closed door."

################################################################################
## CHAPTER 18 — Red Tape and Unforetold Recordings
################################################################################

translate en b18_001ab:
    "The rain eased into a lazy drizzle. Under the shelter of the tin roof's overhang, Yara carefully wiped the mist off her recorder with a microfiber cloth."

translate en b18_001c:
    "The train recording ended. Yara switched off her recorder and carefully wiped the mist clinging to its casing with a microfiber cloth."

translate en b18_002:
    "Kai watched Yara's hands — then his gaze dropped to her half-open canvas bag. Tucked between spare cables and batteries was something he knew all too well. A clear plastic case with a vivid red trim running along its edge."

translate en b18_003:
    kai_hati "That's… a Type IV cassette case. Metal. Pitch-black magnetic tape, metal particles. The exact same kind in Pak Ageng's glass case. The ones money can't buy."

translate en b18_004:
    kai "Yar. {i}(jerks his chin hesitantly toward her bag){/i} That red cassette case in your bag… Are you a Gema Masa regular too?"

translate en b18_005:
    "Yara looked up. She glanced at Kai, then down at her bag. Instead of looking startled, she gave a faint, knowing smile. Reaching inside, she pulled out the blank-labeled red cassette and spun it between her fingers."

translate en b18_006:
    yara "So you go there a lot too? {i}(chuckles){/i} Figures. That first day we met in the library, you looked pale as someone waiting for the Grim Reaper to show up."

translate en b18_007:
    kai "{i}(rubs the back of his neck, flushing){/i} I-I was panicking back then. I heard a full minute of dead silence for Friday. I thought… I thought I was going to die."

translate en b18_008:
    yara "Honestly. Pak Ageng really does love letting people get tangled up in their own assumptions."

translate en b18_009:
    kai "Wait, Yar… those red cassettes don't cost money. Pak Ageng asks for your happiest memory as payment. Did you actually—"

translate en b18_010:
    yara "{i}(grips the red cassette tightly){/i} I did give up one of my memories for this tape, Kai. Just not to predict the future."

translate en b18_011:
    kai "Then what for?"

translate en b18_012:
    yara "To record. {i}(explains, tapping the cassette lightly){/i} Pak Ageng has a stash of top-grade blank Type IV Metal tapes he's never loaded with future frequencies. I bought it because metal tape has the widest dynamic range and the lowest noise floor. It captures the world's sound exactly as it is. Honest. Zero distortion."

translate en b18_013:
    "Yara leaned her back against the warung's wooden post, gazing out at the rain-slicked rails."

translate en b18_014:
    yara "People go to Pak Ageng's shop because they're scared of tomorrow. They want guarantees. Want a heads-up so they can brace themselves. {i}(pause){/i} To me, predicting the future is the fastest way to ruin what's good about today. If you already know how the story ends… what's the point of living through it?"

translate en b18_015:
    kai_hati "It hit me like a slow-motion collision. All this time I've been chasing tomorrow's shadow just to feel safe. Meanwhile she's… standing right where she is. Recording today."

translate en b18_016:
    yara "{i}(looks straight at Kai){/i} I didn't trade my memory to peek at tomorrow, Kai. I did it so I'd have the best medium to hold onto the moments happening right now. Recording the present matters way more than predicting a future that might not even happen."

translate en b18_017:
    "Yara scrolled through the track list on her recorder, as if something had just crossed her mind."

translate en b18_018:
    yara "Oh yeah. Listen to this for a sec. Remember what you told me was the sound you hate most?"

translate en b18_019a:
    yara "Same school bell. Except I recorded it from the field, late afternoon, once school had cleared out. Sounds different, doesn't it? Not like an order. More like… a reminder to head home."

translate en b18_020a:
    kai "{i}(goes quiet for a moment){/i} …Yeah. This one doesn't make my chest tighten up."

translate en b18_019b:
    yara "That's a kid from the class next door, whispering encouragement to a friend right before a presentation. Whispers aren't always gossip, Kai. Sometimes a voice is just too precious to shout."

translate en b18_020b:
    kai "{i}(can't help cracking a small smile){/i} …You really record stuff like that?"

translate en b18_019c:
    yara "This is silence. Recorded in an empty classroom after last period. Listen closely — silence isn't actually empty. It just speaks softly."

translate en b18_020c:
    kai "{i}(presses the headphones tighter for a long moment){/i} …There's sound in there. Super faint. Definitely there, though."

translate en b18_021:
    yara "{i}(nods){/i} Every sound you hate — you just haven't heard it from the right distance yet."

translate en b18_022:
    "Kai looked down at his own recorder — well, his phone. Sitting inside was the one file he'd recorded in the canteen: the boiling pot, his very first recording."

translate en b18_023:
    kai_hati "That boiling pot file. Rough recording, tons of background bleed. Still, it was the first time I recorded something not out of fear… just because it sounded good."

translate en b18_024a:
    kai "Yar. Here… that boiling pot file from the canteen. My recording's pretty bad, honestly. If you want it, though, it's yours."

translate en b18_025a:
    yara "{i}(takes it, gives it a quick listen, then nods firmly){/i} Tons of noise. You nailed the rhythm, though. {i}(types something into her portfolio list){/i} Going in the archive. This is your first recording, Kai. You can't ever redo a first."

translate en b18_024b:
    kai_hati "No. I'll hang onto this one for now. Small proof that I once recorded something just because I wanted to, not because I was scared."

translate en b18_025b:
    yara "{i}(glances over without asking){/i} Keeping something for yourself? {i}(smiles){/i} Good. Not every recording has to be shared."

translate en b18_024c:
    kai "{i}(deletes the file){/i} Gone. It was awful. Way too much bleed."

translate en b18_025c:
    yara "{i}(shrugs){/i} Kind of a shame. Your file, though. Whoever hits record gets the final say."

translate en b18_026:
    "Under the darkening Malang sky, beside tracks left quiet after the old diesel had passed, Kai gazed at the red cassette in Yara's hand — and for the very first time, it didn't look scary at all."

################################################################################
## CHAPTER 19 — Dust Settling on the Shelves
################################################################################

translate en b19_001:
    "There was a small change in Kai's room he only noticed that day. His desk was no longer buried under red-circled calendars and worst-case notes for tomorrow."

translate en b19_001_p2:
    "Now, tucked in the corner, sat a list of sound-hunting spots from Yara, a few spare AA batteries, and a single black hairpin she'd left behind when they were packing up cables the other day."

translate en b19_002:
    "His desk drawer — where he used to stash his forecast cassettes — was starting to gather dust."

translate en b19_003:
    kai_hati "How long has it been since I went to that alley? Two weeks? Three? {i}(smiles faintly){/i} Every morning when I wake up, my first thought isn't \"what's going to happen today\" anymore. It's \"what are we recording today?\""

translate en b19_004:
    "A light drizzle dampened Malang that afternoon. Kai grabbed his jacket. Not out of anxiety. Not because he needed a guarantee for tomorrow. He just… missed the shop."

translate en b19_005:
    "The door chime tinkled softly. The familiar scent of warm tea and old paper greeted him. Pak Ageng sat behind the counter, reading glasses perched on the tip of his nose, cleaning a cassette player with a cotton swab."

translate en b19_006:
    ageng "{i}(looks up, smiling so wide the lines at the corners of his eyes deepen){/i} Ah. The missing time traveler. {i}(chuckles){/i} I thought you'd moved out of town, Kai."

translate en b19_007:
    kai "{i}(laughs lightly — no awkwardness at all this time){/i} Sorry, Pak. I've just… been kind of busy lately."

translate en b19_008:
    ageng "{i}(slides a warm cup of jasmine tea across to Kai){/i} Busy living today, hm? {i}(his gaze flicks down to Kai's jacket pocket, where the tip of a mic cable sticks out){/i}"

translate en b19_009:
    kai "{i}(nods, taking a sip of tea){/i} Yeah, Pak. It feels weird. Back then, if I didn't know what tomorrow held, I couldn't even sleep. Now… I can't even remember the last time I worried about tomorrow."

translate en b19_010:
    ageng "That's because you've found your anchor, kid. People who keep staring ahead usually just don't have anywhere solid to stand in the present. Once you find something — or someone — that makes right now worth holding onto, tomorrow stops feeling like a threat."

translate en b19_011:
    "Kai glanced at the shelves beside the counter. Fine dust had settled over the rows of cassette cases — the shop was aging quietly right along with its owner. Pak Ageng followed his gaze."

translate en b19_012a:
    kai "Pak, let me help dust the shelves. It's getting pretty thick here."

translate en b19_013a:
    ageng "{i}(chuckles){/i} Monggo. The feather duster's behind the counter."

translate en b19_014a:
    "Kai dusted the shelves row by row. On the very bottom row, tucked behind a stack of regular tapes, sat one unlabeled cassette. Its shell had yellowed with age. Kai lifted it gently."

translate en b19_015a:
    kai "Pak, what tape is this? Doesn't have a label."

translate en b19_016a:
    ageng "{i}(goes quiet for a beat; his voice softens){/i} That's… rain. Rain from ninety-four. {i}(pause){/i} Later. I'll tell you about it later."

translate en b19_017a:
    kai_hati "Ninety-four. Did Pak Ageng's hand just… shake a little? First time I've ever seen that."

translate en b19_012b:
    "Kai traced the shelves with his eyes only, reading handwritten labels half-faded by time. He didn't touch a thing. Some things in this shop didn't feel like they were meant to be disturbed."

translate en b19_013b:
    ageng "Those shelves hold people's years, kid. The dust is part of what's inside too."

translate en b19_012c:
    kai "Pak, the one in the corner… is that a broken tape? Half the ribbon's tangled up. If you're not using it, can I buy it cheap?"

translate en b19_013c:
    ageng "{i}(peeks over, then waves a hand){/i} Just take it. Free. A tangled tape can't hold anything anymore — suits you right now. You're not hoarding fear anymore anyway."

translate en b19_018:
    ageng "{i}(gestures toward the glass cabinet beside him, where the Type I forecast tapes sit lined up neatly){/i} So… I take it you didn't come today to buy insurance for tomorrow?"

translate en b19_019:
    "Kai looked at the rows of tapes. The feeling was unfamiliar — and light."

translate en b19_020:
    kai "{i}(shakes his head firmly){/i} No, Pak. I think… I don't need those anymore."

translate en b19_021:
    ageng "{i}(nods, deeply pleased){/i} Hm. Good. Very good, kid."

################################################################################
## CHAPTER 20 — Severed Greed
################################################################################

translate en b20_001:
    "The calm shattered. The wooden door swung open hard enough to make the bell jangle in panic. A middle-aged man stepped inside: sharp, clearly expensive suit, heavy cologne instantly drowning out the scent of jasmine tea and old paper."

translate en b20_001_p2:
    "His breathing was fast. His eyes were bloodshot — not from lack of sleep, but from impatient ambition. Yet when he spoke, his voice came out low and controlled. That was what sent a chill down Kai's spine."

translate en b20_002:
    kaya "Excuse me. {i}(gives a thin, joyless smile){/i} You're the owner of this shop, aren't you?"

translate en b20_003:
    ageng "{i}(slowly sets down his reading glasses){/i} I am. How may I help you, sir?"

translate en b20_004:
    "The man opened his leather briefcase and dropped several thick stacks of hundred-thousand-rupiah bills onto the teak counter. They landed with a heavy thud. An absurd amount of money — worth way more than hundreds of cassettes in this shop."

translate en b20_005:
    kaya "I've heard what this shop sells. And I don't like wasting time on small talk. {i}(scans the cassette shelves){/i} I want to buy every tape for the next six months. Stock movements. Investment calls. Market charts — before any of it happens. {i}(smiles){/i} I want to buy shares in tomorrow, Pak."

translate en b20_006:
    kai_hati "Six months…? I used these tapes to survive math quizzes and classroom embarrassment. This guy wants to use them for… the stock market?"

translate en b20_007a:
    kai "{i}(stands up from his chair, voice shaking but pushing the words out anyway){/i} P-Pak. Excuse me. This shop doesn't sell stuff like that. You've got the wrong place—"

translate en b20_008a:
    "The wealthy man turned his head. For the first time, he really looked at Kai — from his school uniform down to the mic cable peeking out of his jacket pocket. His stare lingered one second longer than felt comfortable."

translate en b20_009a:
    kaya "{i}(softly, taking a mental note){/i} …You're the kid who comes here a lot, aren't you."

translate en b20_010a:
    kai_hati "He said it quietly. Still felt like my name just got written down in his notebook."

translate en b20_011a:
    ageng "{i}(calmly cutting in){/i} This boy is my guest, sir. Your business is with me."

translate en b20_007b:
    "Kai held his breath in his chair, fingers gripping the wooden edge. He looked at the stacks of cash, then at Pak Ageng, then back at the cash — and didn't say a word."

translate en b20_007c:
    "Kai stayed quiet — not because he was frozen. He listened. To every word. Because a small part of his mind, the part that used to buy peace of mind at this counter every afternoon, wanted to know: how much does a man like this think the future is actually worth?"

translate en b20_012:
    ageng "{i}(looks down at the stacks of cash with an ice-cold stare, not a flicker of interest in his eyes){/i} This shop does not sell the future in exchange for numbers on paper, sir. Gema Masa exists to help lost souls find peace. Not to feed greed."

translate en b20_013:
    kaya "{i}(his smile vanishes; his voice stays low, now pressing hard){/i} Pak. Everything in this world has a price. Name yours. {i}(pulls a checkbook from his leather bag){/i} If this cash isn't enough, I can write a check for any number you want. Anything."

translate en b20_014c:
    kai_hati "The kind of numbers he's throwing around… if I'd heard an offer like that a year ago, maybe I— no. Just listen. Listen all the way through, just to know what it feels like to be offered that."

translate en b20_015:
    ageng "This shop refuses to serve you. {i}(rises straight up from his rocking chair){/i} Please take your money back, and leave."

translate en b20_016:
    "Right there, the man's politeness cracked."

translate en b20_017:
    kaya "Don't act all high and mighty with me, old man! {i}(his face turns bright red){/i} Everybody has a price! You included—"

translate en b20_018:
    ageng "{i}(doesn't raise his voice in the slightest){/i} Please leave, sir."

translate en b20_019:
    "The wealthy man hissed under his breath. His eyes locked onto a cassette resting near Pak Ageng's hand. Without warning, he snatched it."

translate en b20_020:
    kaya "If you won't sell it, I'll take it myself!"

translate en b20_021:
    kai "{i}(claps his hands over his ears){/i} Pak—?! The tape—!"

translate en b20_022:
    "The dark brown magnetic tape inside pulled taut, vibrating violently — then snapped clean in two right down the middle. The loose ends curled inward in a dead tangle. The ringing stopped at once, and silence fell back over the shop. The wealthy man recoiled, dropping the ruined cassette onto the counter, his face draining of color."

translate en b20_023:
    ageng "{i}(completely calm, which only makes it cut sharper){/i} The future cannot be bought with greed. That tape snapped because it refused to record a dirty soul. {i}(pause){/i} Now leave. Before this shop closes its doors to you for good."

translate en b20_024:
    "Hands trembling and breath ragged, the man scooped up his stacks of cash and bolted out the door, disappearing into the thickening rain of the dead-end alley."

translate en b20_025:
    "Inside the shop, silence drifted back down like settling dust. Kai stared at the cassette with the snapped tape on the counter for a long, long time."

translate en b20_026:
    kai_hati "I used to come here to control tomorrow too. Only difference is… I was scared, he was greedy. And the tape doesn't care what the difference is — push it too hard, and it snaps for both of us."

translate en b20_027:
    ageng "{i}(sits back down, letting out a quiet sigh as he pours himself some tea){/i} Your tea's gone cold, kid. Let me pour you a fresh cup."

translate en b20_028c:
    "That night, the numbers from the shop wouldn't leave Kai's head. He pulled open his desk drawer — his old forecast tapes were still sitting there — and without meaning to, he started doing the math. How much this tape cost. How much that one cost. Every tape on Pak Ageng's shelves, if sold to a guy like that, how much would it all add up to."

translate en b20_029c:
    kai_hati "{i}(closes the drawer slowly){/i} …No. Enough. Those numbers aren't my business anymore. {i}(pause){/i} Funny thing is, though, now I know exactly why Pak Ageng never puts a price tag on that one."
